A||||17||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Why are peolpe ingoring the 1984 automatics?||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-21-2000||01:42 AM||||I just found a matched pair of 1984 white autmatic fieros with camel/brown/tan interiors.  $1000 total.<P>The first has a professionally rebuilt engine (&lt;200miles) and brakes, but the body and interior are in extrememly fair condition.  And the second has a nice interior and straight body, but runs poorly/doesn't run that well.  I will use parts from the second to improve the first.  <P>I am really excited about having such an ideal starting point for my project, but I am shocked by how cheaply and easily this has come together in one week's trying.  <P>I assume that I have two of the most common Fieros, but why can I get them SO cheap and with so little effot?  <P>Even the relatively rare Indy versions are no more than $1500 lately.  In seven short years, the Fieros will start to be classics.  The 1984s will always be the first-year cars.  <P>What's your opinion on the shockingly low prices for 1984s?  Go ahead and rant, get it out of your system . . .||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||12-21-2000||03:12 AM||||possibly a combinatin of them all being 4cyls., the first year fire problems and bad rep it caused, and that so many were made that first year ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||12-21-2000||08:06 AM||||I think 1984's had more problems than later years.  (Typical first model year.)  '85 and later cars incorporated developmental changes as well as new deck lid, wiring, <I>etc</I>., to accept a V6 engine.<P>And 4-cylinder automatics are slow.  Not slamming your car -- I have a 4-cylinder,too -- but lots of guys want to go faster.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||82-T/A||12-21-2000||08:43 AM||||Hey, don't let it bother you. <P>I have an ALL original 1984 Fiero 2m4 SE.<P>It's fully loaded, WS6, GX3 auto, cruise, defog, sunroof, tilt, power windows, power mirrors, power door locks, extra light package, it's got the really nice seats with the elastic pockets sewn into the back... it's got more options than you can shake a stick at, and of course, the auto.<P>I absolutely LOVE the car. The motor is tired and worn... and the suspension is shot to hell.. but as soon as I can find the time, I'll start with the suspension and have the motor professionally rebuilt with a new Sealed Power performance cam... higher lifter rocker arms.. the works. When I'm done, all the mods will be internal... it will LOOK completely stock, but run low 16s or high 15s. Thats all I'm shooting for with this car. <P>I have a V6 Fiero too, but for some reason, the style of this 1984 red Fiero is just awesome... I personally prefer the look of my 84.. I'd NEVER sell it.. <P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||12-21-2000||08:49 AM||||I'd say you found a great deal, Lucky.<P>However, I think 84s are so cheap because of simple supply and demand.  About 1 out of every 3 Fieros ever made was an 84, so they're plentiful.  And I think it's true that they were more trouble-prone than the later models.  My 84 had lots of electrical problems that took me a long time to debug.  It left me stranded several times (once requiring a 3-mile walk in a driving snowstorm), always due to problems like blown fusible links.  It also went through starters like mad (4 starters in 4 years.)<P>On the plus side, 84's were the car that started it all.  They were also more optioned-out than the 4-bangers of later years, for the most part.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||12-21-2000||09:21 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/finishstage9.gif"> <P>Ok, I'll tell you, only if you won't tell anyone mine is an '84 SE. j/k<P>Yep, many around, many have problems that need to be worked out, "bad rep" etc.<P>Shhh...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Fformula88||12-21-2000||05:14 PM||||Not only are 84's plentiful, but they are the oldest, and that makes a big difference.  Being older, they would tend to have more miles and be in worse condition.  Remember, '84's are 17 model years old, where as 88's are 13.  Although not a big difference, it sounds like a big one.<P>They were also cheaper new than later models.  Since they were less costly to begin with, they would be less costly now.<P>Best thing is there are a lot of good buys on 84's to be had!  Just 'cause its cheap doesn't mean its not any good!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||12-21-2000||06:20 PM||||Hey...they ignore 85-87 four cylinder automatics too...I got my '86 for $500, and that's probably not uncommon.<P>Everything works...now that's really uncommon.||||reg||1
Z||000008||82-T/A||12-21-2000||09:34 PM||||Speaking of 84s... check out this beauty..<P>Unfortunately.. the main topic in the picture was a silly riding mower.. heh, but you can see my 1984 Fiero 2m4 SE in plain site behind it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.ezshots.com/members/82tafiero/images/82tafiero-16.jpg"> <P>THe car is absolutely immaculate... hard to imagine that the engine is worn and the suspension is shot.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||12-21-2000||11:14 PM||||I'm not ignoring 84 autos, in fact I'd love to find one for my sister... I'd get the engine completely rebuilt and make sure it was safe and wouldn't catch fire.. she wants it dark metallic green, with a black leather and carbon fiber interior.. GT exhaust, tinted windows, with front and rear aero fascias.. (her favorite model is the 86SE)<BR>and for safety I'd want to get her better brakes, new bushings and hoses, etc..<P>and she insists on a stereo at least as good as mine... LOL!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||6
Z||000010||baldlobo||12-21-2000||11:19 PM||||I'm not ignorring them either(I really have to take a pic of her  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), 2M4 Auto(that someone screwed with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">)<P>ps: I just found out that the TH125C is only suppost to support 200 Ft/lbs.<p>[This message has been edited by baldlobo (edited 12-21-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||StuGood||12-22-2000||06:03 PM||||Y'mean they <B>made</B> automatic Fieros ??<BR>BTW, which is faster - the red Fiero in the pic, or the lawn tractor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ?<P>I know: hearty har, har.  You <B>said</B> get it out of my system  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>I think the earlier posts hit on the main reasons: the 1984 models are more common (highest sales that year - 100k+ units, I believe).  That, plus the fact that they represent the first production model year (startup problems) and are just plain older that the rest, generally translates into more problems.  IMO, the reason automatics get overlooked, has to do with the idea that "a <B>real</B> sports car (whatever <B>that</B> means) is supposed to be have a <B>clutch</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">"<P>On the other hand, some of the most eye-catching Fieros I've seen in my area are 1984's.  Or, just look at Tigger's car, for example.  It's a beautiful car - reflecting a lot of care and hard work, I'm sure.  <P>If you want to feel better about owning an automatic, just read some of the posts regarding bleeding the hydraulic clutch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.   <BR>      ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||12-22-2000||08:32 PM||||Hmmm, the only reason 84s catch a lot of eyes around here is because they are engulfed in flames...<P>J/K!  84s around here are generally NOT taken care of and in really bad shape.  I don't think I would ever own an 84, it had a lot of electrical bugs.  However, if it wasn't for an 84 SE in mint condition that a friend of mine picked up for 1000 bucks, I would probably never even know anything about Fiero's.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000013||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-23-2000||12:15 AM||||I started this thread asking about why the 1984s with automatics are so cheap.<P>Well, I just drove car #1 home (rebuilt engine, bad body and interior) and now I really can't understand why it was only$900.  The 4cylinder engine pulls up hills like they were not even there.  I think the car is going to be a treasured member of our family.  I love it already.<P>The suspension is pretty loose, at times the wind and the road wrestled control of the car away from me.  It seems the front shocks are gone.  Probably needs bushings and ball joints too, but I expect all of that.  Pleasant surprise:  only 90K miles on the odometer!  200 miles on rebuilt engine (it pulls and purrs, yippie!)<P>Tomorrow I pick up car #2.  It doesn't run, but the body and interior are pretty well maintained.  I will use this as a parts car to make car #1 look as good as that engine runs.  <P>I have no regrets buying a 1984 4cylinder.  If anything, I now understand the allure of the Fiero.  It's half way between a chevette and a ferrari.  God bless these wonderful little cars.<P>And, best of, I am grateful for all the new friends I have madein Pennock's Fiero Forum.  You make owning a Fiero an experience to be shared.  You're  the best.  <P>I can't believe I own a Fiero!  YES!||||reg||1
Z||000014||82-T/A||12-25-2000||08:34 AM||||Hahah StuGod<P>"BTW, which is faster - the red Fiero in the pic, or the lawn tractor  ?"<P><BR>Daymn, thats harsh.<P>That Fiero is pretty slow. It's absolutely got every option you could possibly imagine except for the suede seats. It's got the next level seats instead. That plus the fact that it's an automatic and has over 135k miles on it or something.. it's pretty slow. I figure it does the quarter in something like 18 seconds right now. I know they do it quicker stock, but this ones tired. <P>I plan on doing a few internal mods.. just stuff to get it running faster but nothing that will alter the way it looks.<P>I will be getting the SEALED POWER camshaft from the Fiero Store which should boost hp by around 15-20 hp. I'll get the roller tipped rocker arms from Crane Cams.... K&N air filter just for the hell of it, a slightly better ignition coil for a more complete burn (not that it would really take that much advantage of it anyway) and paint it black to look like the stock one. Then I guess thats it. I'll probably do some porting and polishing. I figure I'll probably be running low 16s POSSIBLY maybe high 15s but I seriously doubt that. I think STOCK with full compression it's supposed to run mid 17s?<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jake||12-25-2000||05:25 PM||||A fiero is a fiero no matter how ya look at it in my opinion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I like the fact that the previous owners didn't have to keep their fiero's garage kept to keep the body in nice shape and free from rust. Most of em can look brand new with a good coat of paint..and you can save that extra money that was gonna be used for dent repair  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They all can look great. But there is still the fact that the 84 is the oldest and in alot of cases in worst mechanical condintion compared to the others. Still...whatever year they are, they can always turn my head and thats for sure!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||terryk||12-25-2000||08:11 PM||||I have a pair of 86SE's and there are good days and bad, but they never fail to make me smile when I'm in them or just staring at them. ||||reg||14
Z||000017||82-T/A||12-25-2000||10:16 PM||||I hold the 84 Fiero completely seperate from the other Fieros. IMHO, while it did have a short life... the 84 Fieros are what started it all. Other than quality issues, the attention to detail on the 84 Fieros IMHO is much greater than nearly all the other years. The word Fiero is found ALL OVER the car, everywhere from the "body by fischer" pieces.. to everything.. things like.. speakers in the headrest, the elastic pockets behind the seats... just soo many more things... the 84 was the TRUE original.. and IMHO, one of the best looking.<P>I'd never think of hot rodding the car.... I plan to keep it stock looking aside from teh mods I have listed in the two posts above. <P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
